<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrea Doria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1466">1466</a>–<a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doria-family">Doria family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrea Doria was a Genoese admiral and statesman, the leading naval commander of his age. In 1528 he switched from French to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> service, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>'s independence under a new aristocratic constitution and became its dominant figure for three decades. As admiral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs, and he survived the Fieschi conspiracy against him in 1547.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrea-doria.jpg" alt="Andrea Doria" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebastiano del Piombo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Doria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrea Doria was born on 30 November 1466 at Oneglia, into a branch of the old Genoese Doria family. Orphaned young, he became a soldier of fortune, serving first in the papal guard and then under various Italian princes. His reputation led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a>, the leading general of the age, to try to recruit him. In 1503 he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> for the Genoese navy, then under French overlordship.</p><p>After the Battle of Ravenna in 1512 he turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>'s pro-French government and, at 46 and with no naval experience, was made admiral. He helped expel the French and install Ottaviano Fregoso as doge, and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates, sacking the base of the brothers Aruj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-goulette">La Goulette</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Doria later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> and in June 1526 defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> off Civitavecchia. Dissatisfied with Francis's treatment and payment, he went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in June 1528. Charles made him grand admiral, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a> and Marquis of Tursi. In September 1528 Doria drove the French out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>.</p><p>In 1532 his Spanish and Genoese fleet took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> and sacked Patras, and the next year he relieved Coron against a much larger Ottoman fleet. In 1535 he shared command with Álvaro de Bazán the Elder in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, though Barbarossa escaped. Given command of the Holy League in 1538, he blockaded Barbarossa near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, but the allied fleet was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-preveza">Battle of Preveza</a> that September. Venetian accounts blamed Doria for lack of initiative; imperial accounts said the Venetians disobeyed him. The Venetian version became the prevailing one. His 1541 expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, urged on him against his advice in autumn, was wrecked by a storm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, Doria reformed the constitution along aristocratic lines, ending the factions of earlier centuries and organising the ruling class into 28 Alberghi. The doge's term was cut to two years, and commoners were barred from the office. He refused the lordship and the dogeship, accepting the title Father of the Fatherland and the post of perpetual censor, and in effect ruled through his wealth and military power. His constitution lasted until 1797. He blocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s attempts to build a citadel garrisoned by Spaniards in the city.</p><p>In 1547 the Fieschi conspiracy killed his nephew Giannettino, but it was crushed, and Doria punished the conspirators harshly. He put to sea again at 84 and died on 25 November 1560. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-kamen">Henry Kamen</a> regarded him as the most distinguished admiral of his time, and he helped develop amphibious warfare. Several ships have borne his name, among them the liner SS Andrea Doria, which sank after a collision in 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrea Doria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1528 <b>Andrea Doria</b>’s galleys served Charles V against the Ottomans and the Barbary corsairs, but in later centuries Genoa’s small navy could not protect it from the French bombardment of 1684.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1528 <b>Andrea Doria</b> gave Genoa a constitution that reserved government to its enrolled nobility.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oneglia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.89,8.04&amp;year=1527" aria-label="Oneglia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4743 609 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrea Doria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5193 879h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5193 879h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.89,8.04&amp;year=1527">What was here in 1527? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
